(window.LADDER_CARDS["4·comp"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["4·comp"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"4·comp.1.1","q":"What is the mark of a settled statement?","a":"1 if it is so, 0 if it is not","w":["0 if it is so, 1 if it is not","any number at all","its number of letters"],"why":"The mark is a sift of 1 by the statement: all of 1, or nothing. Settled, it is one or the other."},
 {"u":"4·comp.2.1","q":"Why is a network of switches the same object as an expression in marks?","a":"switches one after the other are an and, side by side an or","w":["each switch is a number past 1","a network has no inputs","wires are statements"],"why":"So the techniques simplify a network: the simpler expression is a cheaper network that does the same job."},
 {"u":"4·comp.3.1","q":"(A ⊕ B) + 2 · A · B = A + B. Why does XOR need the overlap twice, where OR needs it once?","a":"on the row where both are 1, XOR gives 0, so the overlap must make up the whole 2","w":["XOR gives 2 on that row","OR gives 0 on that row","the overlap is never 1"],"why":"OR gives 1 there, so one overlap makes up the 2."},
 {"u":"4·comp.4.1","q":"In a two-bit adder, which gate gives the carry and which the sum bit?","a":"AND gives the carry and XOR the sum bit","w":["OR gives the carry and AND the sum bit","XOR gives the carry and OR the sum bit","NAND gives both"],"why":"A + B = 2 · (A · B) + (A ⊕ B): the outputs, read as a numeral in twos, are the sum."},
 {"u":"4·comp.5.1","q":"Why may the full adder’s two carries g and q be joined by an OR gate?","a":"they never both fire, so g ∨ q = g + q","w":["OR and + agree on every row","g is always 0","q is always 1"],"why":"When g = 1, the two bits are both 1, so their sum bit is 0 and q = 0."},
 {"u":"4·comp.6.1","q":"Four bits are kept and 9 + 8 is added. What does the last carry out mean?","a":"it is the top bit of the sum; dropped, the machine reports 1: overflow","w":["it is always 0","it is the sum bit of the first place","it means the sum is 0"],"why":"Each place’s carry out is wired to the next carry in, so the chain adds numerals in twos of any length, by induction."},
 {"u":"4·comp.7.1","q":"Why is NAND alone enough to build every gate?","a":"NOT, AND and OR can each be built from it, and every table from those","w":["NAND is the same as AND","every table has one row","NAND has no inputs"],"why":"nand(A, A) is ¬A. Marks are settled, so 1f’s techniques run both ways."},
 {"u":"4·comp.8.1","q":"Why can two different circuits compute one table?","a":"a table fixes the outputs, and different expressions can have the same column","w":["tables are not settled","one of the two must be wrong","a circuit has no table"],"why":"1e’s recipe: an and for each row whose output is 1, joined by ors. Then the techniques simplify it."},
 {"u":"4·comp.9.1","q":"Why is “S = A ∨ B” for the sum bit a move to watch out for?","a":"on the row where both are 1 the sum bit is 0, and OR gives 1","w":["OR is not a gate","the sum bit is always 1","A ∨ B is not a mark"],"why":"The sum bit is XOR."});
