(window.LADDER_CARDS["8·comp"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["8·comp"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"8·comp.1.1","q":"An s-bit machine keeps 0 and ±m · 2^k with s bits for m. How are its kept numbers spaced?","a":"evenly between two powers of two, and the spacing doubles at each power","w":["evenly along the whole line","closer together for larger numbers","at every fraction with bottom 10"],"why":"If 2ⁿ ≤ x < 2ⁿ⁺¹ the gap at x is 2^(n+1−s). The machine keeps the sign, the power and the first bits."},
 {"u":"8·comp.2.1","q":"Why has one tenth no ending numeral in twos?","a":"it would be m/2^k, so 10m = 2^k and 5 would divide a power of 2","w":["because 10 is even","because one tenth is too small","because the machine has too few bits"],"why":"A numeral in twos that ends after k places names some m/2^k. No count of bits holds 1/10."},
 {"u":"8·comp.3.1","q":"What does the half-gap technique say?","a":"the kept number is never farther from the true one than half a gap","w":["the kept number is always below the true one","the kept number is exact for every fraction","the error is one whole gap at least"],"why":"On a 53-bit machine that is within 2⁻⁵³ ≈ 1.1 · 10⁻¹⁶ of the number’s own size. Close, and not nothing."},
 {"u":"8·comp.4.1","q":"The machine adds st(0.1) and st(0.2), then keeps the result. Why can that miss st(0.3)?","a":"the sum carries three roundings, up to three half-gaps, while st(0.3) carries one","w":["the machine adds wrongly","0.3 has an ending numeral in twos","the two roads round in the same direction"],"why":"Adding-gaps: the triangle technique adds the moves. Two roads to three tenths can end on neighbouring kept numbers."},
 {"u":"8·comp.5.1","q":"The Vancouver index was cut to three places after every trade and lost about 574 points. Why?","a":"every cut loses between 0 and 0.001, all the same way, and such losses add up","w":["the trades themselves lost value","rounding errors always cancel","the index had too few digits before the point"],"why":"Over well over a million updates the losses never cancel. Rounding to the nearest would have pointed the moves both ways."},
 {"u":"8·comp.6.1","q":"What should a program test instead of sameness of two computed results?","a":"that they are within a chosen distance, the sum of the bounds on their roads","w":["that they print alike","that both are whole numbers","sameness, after adding more bits"],"why":"Near-enough never calls two roads to one real different. Its price: it may call two different reals the same when they are closer than that distance."},
 {"u":"8·comp.7.1","q":"“More bits make 0.1 exact.” Why not?","a":"no-ending-twos: 1/10 is no m/2^k, whatever the count of bits","w":["64 bits are enough","bits are decimal digits","only odd counts of bits fail"],"why":"More bits shrink the gap and never close it."},
 {"u":"8·comp.8.1","q":"Handed a fraction in lowest terms, how do you tell whether a machine may hold it exactly?","a":"ask whether its bottom has a prime other than 2: if it has, the machine cannot","w":["ask whether its top is even","ask whether it is below 1","ask whether its decimal ends"],"why":"Only bottoms that are powers of two give an ending numeral in twos. 1/10 ends in tens and not in twos."});
/* end phase F und_r08 */
