(window.LADDER_CARDS["12·comp"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["12·comp"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"12·comp.1.1","q":"On this page, what are a string, the empty string and a language, and which old marks get a second job?","a":"a string is a list of letters written without commas, ε is the string of length 0, and a language is any set of strings; ε, the high n in aⁿ, the letter S and the arrow → are reused","w":["a string is a set of letters, ε is a small positive real, and a language is a grammar; no mark is reused","a string is a number in twos, the empty string is 0, and a language is an alphabet; only = is reused","a string is a rule, ε is a letter, and a language is one string; the marks ∀ and ∃ are reused"],"why":"Here ε is only the empty string, aⁿ is a written n times, S is the start placeholder, and u → v reads “may be rewritten as”."},
 {"u":"12·comp.2.1","q":"What is a grammar, which strings are its sentences, and what does the sentences technique say of S → aSb, S → ab?","a":"letters, placeholders, a start placeholder S and a finite list of rules u → v; its sentences are the strings of plain letters the rules reach from S; for these two rules they are exactly aⁿbⁿ with n ≥ 1","w":["a list of strings; its sentences are every string over the alphabet; here they are every string of a's and b's","a machine with states; its sentences are the strings it rejects; here they are aⁿ","a single rule; its sentences are the strings holding S; here they are aSb and ab"],"why":"Reachable is defined by 4d's recursion, one rule applied at a time. Apply S → aSb n − 1 times, then S → ab."},
 {"u":"12·comp.3.1","q":"In what sense does a grammar run 1a's transform backwards?","a":"1a took English as it came and rewrote it into statements; a grammar starts from a few rules and works forward to make sentences: the inverse of reading them","w":["it turns statements back into the same English they came from","it reads strings from right to left","it checks whether a string someone wrote is true"],"why":"From a finite list of rules it reaches endlessly many strings. The rules cannot tell the ones that say something real from the ones that do not."},
 {"u":"12·comp.4.1","q":"What are the four families of grammars, and which wall does this page prove?","a":"regular, context-free, context-sensitive and unrestricted, each allowing more kinds of rule than the one before; the page proves the first wall: aⁿbⁿ is in the second family and not the first","w":["short, long, finite and endless grammars; the page proves the last wall","grammars with one, two, three and four rules; the page proves they are all equal","regular and irregular only; the page proves no wall"],"why":"Each family's languages are languages of the next, and each next family has languages the one before lacks. The first family is what a machine with no memory checks."},
 {"u":"12·comp.5.1","q":"What is a machine with no memory, and what does remainder-machine say?","a":"finitely many states, a start state, accepting states and a table giving the next state for each state and letter; with three states it finds the remainder by 3 of any number written in twos","w":["a machine that stores the whole string; it divides any number by 3","a table of all strings; it has one state for each number","a grammar with no rules; it accepts every string"],"why":"It remembers only the state it is in. Reading a digit doubles the number and adds the digit, and the remainder by 3 follows along."},
 {"u":"12·comp.6.1","q":"What does step-walk say, and why is its proof easier than in-between-value's?","a":"a walk of integers that moves by one at each step passes through every integer between its start and its end; no integer lies strictly between two neighbours, so there is nothing to fall through and no least upper bound is needed","w":["a walk never returns to its start; the proof is one line of algebra","every walk ends at 0; the proof uses no-holes twice","a walk passes every real between its ends; the proof is the same as 12a's"],"why":"The proof is an induction on the number of steps. So a walk that goes below 0 hits −1 first, and a bracket checker need only stop at −1."},
 {"u":"12·comp.7.1","q":"What do bounded-depth and no-memory-wall say?","a":"for each k, a machine with k + 2 states checks the balanced strings whose depth never rises above k; and no machine with no memory checks aⁿbⁿ","w":["one machine checks brackets of every depth; and aⁿbⁿ needs k + 2 states","no machine checks brackets of any depth; and every language has a machine","a machine with k states checks depth 2k; and aⁿbⁿ is checked by the remainder machine"],"why":"With q states, two of a⁰, a¹, …, a^q land in the same state by pigeonhole, and the machine cannot tell them apart afterwards. The line it cannot cross is 12b's word: bounded."},
 {"u":"12·comp.8.1","q":"Why is “a machine with more states checks aⁿbⁿ” a move to watch out for?","a":"the proof works for every number q of states: a machine with q states is fooled by some aⁱbⁱ against aʲbⁱ with i < j ≤ q","w":["more states make a machine slower","aⁿbⁿ has only finitely many strings","states cannot be added to a machine"],"why":"The wall is a proof about every machine with no memory, not a report on the ones tried so far."},
 {"u":"12·comp.9.1","q":"Handed a language, how do you tell whether a machine with no memory checks it?","a":"ask what must be known about the letters read so far: if finitely many facts decide every answer, build one state per fact; if endlessly many strings must be told apart, pigeonhole says no such machine exists","w":["count its strings: a finite language has no machine","write a grammar for it: any grammar gives a machine","try machines of growing size until one works"],"why":"For aⁿbⁿ the strings a⁰, a¹, a², … must all be told apart. For the remainder by 3, three facts are enough."});
/* end phase F und_r12 */
