(window.LADDER_CARDS["19·comp"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["19·comp"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"19·comp.1.1","q":"What are R and R′, in which order is a string of twists read, and why do the strings of twists form a group?","a":"R is a quarter turn of the right face, clockwise as you look at it, and R′ is R undone; a string is read in the order done; twists are shuffles of the 54 stickers, strings of them are shuffles, every one can be undone, and doing nothing is the empty string","w":["R is a half turn of the right face and R′ a quarter turn; a string is read right to left; they form a group because twists are turn-about","R is a quarter turn of the right face and R′ is R undone; R U means U first; they form a group because every string has order 4","R and R′ are the same twist seen from two sides; a string is read in the order done; they form a group because there are finitely many stickers"],"why":"R U means R first, then U; as a product in 19a's way of writing it is UR."},
 {"u":"19·comp.2.1","q":"What does the take-apart count say, and how is it counted by stages?","a":"a cube pulled apart can be put back in 8! · 3⁸ · 12! · 2¹² ways, about 5.2 × 10²⁰: corners into corner places, each three ways round, edges into edge places, each two ways round, and the stages multiply","w":["it can be put back in 8! + 3⁸ + 12! + 2¹² ways: the stages add","it can be put back in 8! · 12! ways: a piece's way round does not count","it can be put back in 54! ways: any sticker can go to any place"],"why":"Every twisted cube is one of these; the question is how many of them twisting can reach."},
 {"u":"19·comp.3.1","q":"What are the three things no twist changes, and how do they give the reach count?","a":"the corner and edge shuffles are both even or both odd, the total corner twist is a whole number of turns, and the number of wrong-way edges is even; the 3 · 2 · 2 = 12 sets of values split the ways into at least 12 copies of G, so twisting reaches at most one way in twelve","w":["the centres, the corners and the edges each stay in place; that leaves one way in three","the corner shuffle is always even, the edge shuffle is always even, and no corner is ever twisted; that leaves one way in eight","the parity, the twist total and the wrong-way count each take 2 values; that leaves one way in six"],"why":"519 024 039 293 878 272 000 / 12 = 43 252 003 274 489 856 000, about 4.3 × 10¹⁹."},
 {"u":"19·comp.4.1","q":"What is C = y⁻¹x⁻¹yx, when is it e, and what does the small-change technique say?","a":"do x, do y, undo x, undo y; it is e exactly when x and y turn about; if exactly one place p is moved by both, C moves at most three places: p, x⁻¹(p) and y⁻¹(p)","w":["do x, do y, then undo both in the same order; it is always e; C moves every place that x or y moves","do x, do y, undo x, undo y; it is e exactly when x = y; C moves only p","do x, undo x, do y, undo y; it is never e; C moves at most three places whatever x and y share"],"why":"R′ D′ R U R′ D R U′ sends three corners round a loop, and nothing else moves."},
 {"u":"19·comp.5.1","q":"Why is \"the undo of R U is R′ U′\" a move to watch out for?","a":"by socks and shoes the last twist done is the first undone: the undo is U′ first, then R′","w":["the undo of R U is R U done three more times, and nothing shorter","R and U turn about, so the order does not matter, but the marks should be U′ R′","a string of two twists has no undo"],"why":"R U R′ U′ is not doing nothing: R and U share three pieces, and it moves seven."});
/* end phase F und_r19 */
