(window.LADDER_CARDS["30·comp"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30·comp"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30·comp.1.1","q":"What is the surprise of an outcome, and what does the surprise-average technique say about H(X)?","a":"the surprise of an outcome of chance p is log₂(1/p) bits; H(X), the average surprise, adds over independent parts and is largest, log₂ n, when the n outcomes are equally likely","w":["the surprise is p bits; H multiplies over independent parts and is largest when one outcome is sure","the surprise is log₂ p bits; H is the same for every source with n outcomes","the surprise is log₂(1/p) bits; H adds over independent parts and is largest, n bits, for n equally likely outcomes"],"why":"A sure outcome has surprise 0."},
 {"u":"30·comp.2.1","q":"What does the leaf-count technique say, and what does the source coding technique say H is?","a":"the lengths ℓ of a code in which no codeword begins another satisfy Σ 2^(−ℓ) ≤ 1, and any lengths that satisfy it can be given such a code; H is the least average number of bits an outcome, reached to within 1/n by coding blocks of n","w":["the lengths satisfy Σ ℓ ≤ n; H is the most bits an outcome can need","the lengths satisfy Σ 2^(−ℓ) ≥ 1; H is the average length of the best code for single outcomes, which blocks cannot improve","the lengths satisfy Σ 2^(−ℓ) ≤ 1; H can be beaten by coding long enough blocks"],"why":"H is exactly how far a source can be squeezed."},
 {"u":"30·comp.3.1","q":"What is I(X; Y), what does the shared-information technique say about it, and what is a flip-line’s capacity?","a":"I(X; Y) is the drop in the average surprise of X once Y is known; it is the same either way round, never below 0, and 0 just when X and Y are independent; a line that flips each bit with chance ε has capacity 1 − h(ε) bits a use","w":["I(X; Y) is H(X) + H(Y); it is largest when X and Y are independent; the capacity is 1 − ε","I(X; Y) is the drop in the surprise of X once Y is known; it differs from I(Y; X); the capacity is h(ε)","I(X; Y) is the chance that X = Y; it can fall below 0; the capacity is 1 − 2ε"],"why":"At ε = 1/2 the capacity is 0."},
 {"u":"30·comp.4.1","q":"What does the noisy channel technique promise, and how does its proof show that a code exists without building one?","a":"any rate below the capacity can be carried with errors as rare as you like, by coding long enough blocks; codes drawn at random work on average, so at least one code does as well as the average","w":["any rate can be carried without error by repeating each bit; the proof builds the repeating code","rates below the capacity can be carried with a fixed error rate; the proof checks every code","any rate below the capacity can be carried with rare errors; the proof writes down the best code and counts its errors"],"why":"An average over codes is not above all its values (30g)."},
 {"u":"30·comp.5.1","q":"Why is “a line with ε = 0.6 carries nothing” a move to watch out for?","a":"flip every bit received and the line flips with chance 0.4, so its capacity is 1 − h(0.6) = 1 − h(0.4), which is above 0; only ε = 1/2 carries nothing","w":["a line with ε above 1/2 carries more than one bit a use","a line with ε = 0.6 carries 0.6 bits a use","nothing is wrong; more flips than not means no information"],"why":"A line that is usually wrong is as good as one that is usually right."});
/* end phase F und_r30 */
/* phase F und_r32: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["32·comp"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["32·comp"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"32·comp.1.1","q":"Why is a row of pixels reflected end to end before it is split, and what does the mirrored-row technique say about its energy?","a":"repeated as it stands, the row would jump from its last pixel to its first, with amounts falling like 1/n; reflected, it repeats with no jump and needs cosines alone; the N cosines are at right angles, so with sizes 1 the row’s energy is the sum of the squares of its amounts","w":["reflection doubles the number of pixels kept; the energy of the amounts is twice the row’s","reflection makes the row odd, so it needs sines alone; the energy is the largest amount squared","reflection removes the average; the amounts hold less energy than the row"],"why":"A jump costs 1/n; no jump does better (32h3)."},
 {"u":"32·comp.2.1","q":"How much of the example row’s variation sits in its first three cosines, and why is that a law and not a technique?","a":"about 81 per cent, 286 of 351.5; nothing proves it: a row of random grey levels spreads its energy over all eight, and it holds for photos because the world is smooth almost everywhere, a measured regularity","w":["all of it; it is proved by the energy-kept technique","about 81 per cent; it is proved for every row by the mirrored-row technique","about 8 per cent; it is a law because cameras are built that way"],"why":"True of the photos people take, false of noise."},
 {"u":"32·comp.3.1","q":"What does the rounding-error technique say, and why do most stored numbers come out 0?","a":"rounding each amount to a multiple of its step q_k moves it by at most q_k/2, and the error’s energy in the row is exactly the sum of the squared moves; the fast cosines have small amounts and large steps, so they round to 0","w":["rounding moves each pixel by at most one step; the stored numbers are 0 because the pixels are equal","rounding moves each amount by at most q_k, and the error’s energy is the largest move; the zeros come from the slow cosines","rounding loses no energy; the zeros are added afterwards to pad the file"],"why":"The example stores (11, −1, −1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)."},
 {"u":"32·comp.4.1","q":"Why are the steps for the fast cosines set coarse, and which two losses does the eye still notice?","a":"the eye sees fine stripes poorly, a measured law, so errors there are forgiven; edges ring, as a cut-off sum must by 32c’s overshoot, and at strong compression the 8 by 8 blocks show seams, since each is rounded on its own","w":["fast cosines hold most of the energy; the eye notices lost colour and lost brightness","the eye sees fine stripes best, so they are stored first; nothing is noticed","the steps are set by a technique of 32g; the eye notices only the file’s size"],"why":"The steps follow the eye, not a proof."},
 {"u":"32·comp.5.1","q":"Why is “splitting a row into cosines is what shrinks the file” a move to watch out for?","a":"eight pixels become eight amounts, with the same energy and nothing saved; it is the rounding, which turns most amounts into zeros, that shrinks the file","w":["the split halves the count of numbers, and rounding does the rest","the split loses the fast cosines by itself","the file is not smaller at all after either move"],"why":"The split is undone exactly; the rounding is not."});
/* end phase F und_r32 */
/* phase F und_r36: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["36·comp"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["36·comp"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"36·comp.1.1","q":"What does a machine store for each place of a picture, how do those become numbers in [0, 1], and which two limits does the three-bytes technique put on a screen?","a":"Three whole numbers from 0 to 255, one byte for each primary, each divided by 255; the screen shows exactly 256³ = 16 777 216 colours, all inside the gamut of its primaries: limited once by the triangle and once by the steps","w":["Three numbers from 0 to 1 with endless digits; the screen shows every colour of G","Three bytes, each divided by 256; the screen shows 3 × 256 = 768 colours, limited only by the steps","One byte for the hue; the screen shows 256 colours, limited only by the triangle"],"why":"A byte is eight places in twos, and 2⁸ = 256."},
 {"u":"36·comp.2.1","q":"How does the machine get brightness and saturation from the amounts r, g, b, what does the hue-sector technique give when red is largest and blue smallest, where is the machine’s 0, and what happens at a grey?","a":"Brightness is the largest amount M, and saturation is (M − m)/M with m the smallest; the hue is 60° × (g − b)/(M − m); the machine’s bend puts red at 0, not purple; at a grey the formula is 0/0 and the program writes hue 0, which is harmless","w":["Brightness is the average of the three, and saturation is M − m; the hue is 60° × g; the machine puts purple at 0; at a grey it stops","Brightness is M and saturation is (M − m)/M; the hue is 60° × (g − b)/(M − m); the machine puts red at 0; at a grey the hue is truly 0, red","Brightness is m and saturation is m/M; the hue is 360° × g; the machine puts green at 0; at a grey it writes hue 180°"],"why":"(255, 128, 0): brightness 1, saturation 1, hue about 30°."},
 {"u":"36·comp.3.1","q":"How are multiply and screen worked on bytes, how far can one rounding move a result, and what follows for a chain of blends?","a":"Multiply is c·d/255 and screen is 255 − (255 − c)(255 − d)/255, each rounded to a whole number; a rounding moves the result by at most half a step; over a chain the moves add at most, so ten blends can drift by up to five steps","w":["Multiply is c·d and screen is c + d, with no rounding; chains never drift","Multiply is c·d/255 and screen is 255 − (255 − c)(255 − d)/255; a rounding moves the result by up to a whole step, and the errors cancel over a chain","Multiply is c·d/256 and screen is 256 − c·d; a rounding moves the result by at most half a step, and a chain drifts by half a step in all"],"why":"Multiply of 200 and 100 is 78.43, kept as 78."},
 {"u":"36·comp.4.1","q":"What does a machine’s alpha measure, how does it differ from the rung’s alpha, what does the over-blend technique give, and why does red faded over blue look dull on the way?","a":"How much of the place the layer covers, not brightness; a layer c with alpha α over d gives α·c + (1 − α)·d in each coordinate; that is a mix of lights, a chord between two hues, so it runs inside the ring and desaturates","w":["The layer’s brightness, the same as the rung’s; the blend is c + d; the fade is dull because bytes are rounded","How much of the place the layer covers; the blend is α·c·d; the fade passes through the full purple of the ring","How much of the place the layer covers; the blend is α·c + (1 − α)·d; the fade is dull because alpha lowers the brightness of both"],"why":"(255, 0, 0) at 0.25 over (0, 0, 255) gives (64, 0, 191)."},
 {"u":"36·comp.5.1","q":"Why are bytes written in sixteens, what does the two-digit technique say, and how is #FF8000 read as three amounts?","a":"16 = 2⁴, so four places in twos make exactly one place in sixteens; every byte is written with exactly two digits, each four places of the byte in twos; #FF8000 is red 255, green 128, blue 0","w":["Sixteens are shorter than tens; every byte needs three digits; #FF8000 is red 15, green 8, blue 0","16 = 2⁴; every byte is written with exactly two digits; #FF8000 is red 0, green 128, blue 255","Tens cannot write 255; every byte is one digit; #FF8000 is the one number 16 744 448 and no amounts"],"why":"FF is 15 · 16 + 15, and 80 is 8 · 16 + 0."},
 {"u":"36·comp.6.1","q":"In what way is a picture a function, what does the picture-count technique count, and why can a flip be undone while a shrink cannot?","a":"It sends each place, a pair (i, j) of column and row, to the three bytes of that place; a picture of w columns and h rows takes 3wh bytes and there are 256^(3wh) of them; a flip composes with a one-to-one onto map of the places, while a shrink maps two columns to one and forgets","w":["It sends each colour to the places that hold it; there are 256 · 3wh pictures; a shrink can be undone by stretching","It sends each place to its three bytes; a picture takes wh bytes and there are 3^(wh) pictures; a flip loses the left half","It sends each place to its three bytes; a picture takes 3wh bytes and there are 256^(3wh) of them; neither a flip nor a shrink can be undone"],"why":"Doing the flip twice is the identity."},
 {"u":"36·comp.7.1","q":"Why can a picture of fine stripes show broad stripes that the scene does not have, what does the stripe technique give for stripes every 0.9 places, and why does a sharp edge come out as a staircase?","a":"Sampled once per place, a frequency and that frequency shifted by a whole number give the same samples; stripes every 0.9 places, frequency 1.111…, are read as 0.111…, broad stripes every 9 places; a step has frequencies without end, so no sampling rate is enough","w":["The screen is too dim for fine stripes; stripes every 0.9 places show every 0.9 places; an edge is a staircase because places are round","A frequency and its shift by a whole number give the same samples; stripes every 0.9 places show every 1.8 places; an edge is a staircase only at low brightness","The camera averages each place; stripes every 0.9 places vanish; an edge has one frequency, which the rate misses"],"why":"The same-samples technique of 32g, at rate 1 per place."},
 {"u":"36·comp.8.1","q":"What colour does the averaged picture give a place crossed by an edge, what does the coverage technique say, and why does a k by k average still let some false stripes through?","a":"A colour between the two sides in proportion to what each covers: s·c + (1 − s)·d, the over-blend with alpha s; averaging over the square weakens the frequencies above ½ and does not take them to 0, and k by k samples give only k² + 1 shades","w":["The colour of the side that holds the centre; the coverage technique picks the larger share; averaging removes every false stripe","s·c + (1 − s)·d; a k by k average gives k shades and removes the frequencies above ½ entirely","The pigment mix c·d; the share s plays no part; averaging adds stripes"],"why":"Four by four samples give 17 shades."});
/* end phase F und_r36 */
